---
import SimulatorLayout from '../../layouts/SimulatorLayout.astro';
import CompareSim from '../../simulators/compare-cells/CompareSim.astro';
import { compareCellsQuiz } from '../../data/quizzes/compare-cells';

const toc = [
  { id: 'shared', label: 'What they have in common' },
  { id: 'differences', label: 'The key differences' },
  { id: 'table', label: 'Comparison table' },
  { id: 'why', label: 'Why the differences exist' },
  { id: 'microscope', label: 'Seeing cells under a microscope' },
  { id: 'terms', label: 'Key terms' },
  { id: 'misconceptions', label: 'Common misconceptions' },
  { id: 'takeaways', label: 'Key takeaways' },
];

const references = [
  { text: 'Clark, M. A., Douglas, M., & Choi, J. (2018). Biology 2e, Section 4.3: Eukaryotic Cells. OpenStax.', url: 'https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-3-eukaryotic-cells' },
  { text: 'Clark, M. A., Douglas, M., & Choi, J. (2018). Biology 2e, Section 4.2: Prokaryotic Cells. OpenStax.', url: 'https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-2-prokaryotic-cells' },
  { text: 'Alberts, B., et al. (2022). Molecular Biology of the Cell (7th ed.). W. W. Norton.' },
];
---

<SimulatorLayout
  branch="zoology"
  topic="animal-vs-plant-cell"
  description="Interactive animal cell vs. plant cell comparison: click structures in both cells, highlight the differences and test yourself with a sorting challenge."
  toc={toc}
  quiz={compareCellsQuiz}
  references={references}
>
  <Fragment slot="intro">
    <p>
      An animal cell and a plant cell are built on the same basic plan, yet a few key differences explain why animals
      move and eat while plants stand still and make their own food. Compare them side by side, highlight what sets them
      apart, and sort structures in a quick challenge.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <CompareSim slot="sim" />

  <Fragment slot="instructions">
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Click any structure</strong> in either cell to see its name, its job and which cells contain it.</li>
      <li>Turn on <strong>Highlight the differences</strong>: shared structures fade, plant-only structures are outlined in green and animal-only structures in red.</li>
      <li>Try the <strong>sorting challenge</strong>: press <strong>New question</strong> and decide whether a structure is found in animal cells only, plant cells only, or both.</li>
      <li>Turn labels off to test your memory. <strong>Reset</strong> clears your score.</li>
    </ol>
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="shared">What they have in common</h2>
  <p>
    Animal and plant cells are both <strong>eukaryotic</strong>: their DNA is held inside a nucleus, and they contain
    membrane-bound organelles. Both have a <strong>cell membrane</strong>, <strong>cytoplasm</strong>, a
    <strong>nucleus</strong>, <strong>mitochondria</strong> for respiration, <strong>ribosomes</strong> for making
    proteins, <strong>endoplasmic reticulum</strong> and a <strong>Golgi apparatus</strong>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="differences">The key differences</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Cell wall:</strong> plant cells have a rigid cellulose wall; animal cells do not.</li>
    <li><strong>Chloroplasts:</strong> many plant cells contain chloroplasts for photosynthesis; animal cells never do.</li>
    <li><strong>Vacuole:</strong> plant cells have a large, permanent central vacuole; animal cells have only small, temporary ones.</li>
    <li><strong>Shape:</strong> plant cells are usually regular and box-like; animal cells vary widely in shape.</li>
    <li><strong>Food storage:</strong> plants store starch; animals store glycogen and fat.</li>
    <li><strong>Centrioles and lysosomes:</strong> typical of animal cells and absent from most plant cells.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="table">Comparison table</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Structure</th><th>Animal cell</th><th>Plant cell</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Cell membrane</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Nucleus</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Mitochondria</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Ribosomes, ER, Golgi</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Cell wall</td><td>No</td><td>Yes (cellulose)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Chloroplasts</td><td>No</td><td>Yes (in green parts)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Large central vacuole</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Centrioles</td><td>Yes</td><td>Usually no</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Storage carbohydrate</td><td>Glycogen</td><td>Starch</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="why">Why the differences exist</h2>
  <p>
    The differences match the two lifestyles. Plants are <strong>producers</strong>: they stay in one place and make
    their own food, so they need chloroplasts and a strong wall and turgid vacuole to hold them up toward the light
    without a skeleton. Animals are <strong>consumers</strong>: they eat other organisms and many move around, so their
    cells need no chloroplasts and benefit from flexible membranes that allow varied shapes, such as long nerve cells or
    contracting muscle cells.
  </p>

  <h2 id="microscope">Seeing cells under a microscope</h2>
  <p>
    With a school light microscope you can see the nucleus, cytoplasm, membrane, and in plant cells the wall, vacuole
    and chloroplasts. Onion epidermis cells (which have no chloroplasts, because they grow underground) and human cheek
    cells are classic examples. Smaller organelles such as ribosomes and the details of mitochondria can only be seen
    with an electron microscope.
  </p>

  <h2 id="terms">Key terms</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt>Eukaryotic</dt>
    <dd>Having a nucleus and membrane-bound organelles.</dd>
    <dt>Autotroph and heterotroph</dt>
    <dd>An organism that makes its own food (such as a plant) and one that must eat other organisms (such as an animal).</dd>
    <dt>Turgor</dt>
    <dd>Pressure of a plant cell’s contents against its wall.</dd>
  </dl>

  <h2 id="misconceptions">Common misconceptions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>“Plant cells don’t have mitochondria because they have chloroplasts.”</strong> They have both.</li>
    <li><strong>“All plant cells are green.”</strong> Root and storage cells usually lack chloroplasts.</li>
    <li><strong>“Animal cells have a cell wall too.”</strong> Only plants, fungi and bacteria have walls (made of different materials).</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="takeaways">Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Both cell types share the core eukaryotic organelles.</li>
    <li>Plant cells add a cell wall, chloroplasts and a large central vacuole.</li>
    <li>Animal cells have centrioles and store glycogen rather than starch.</li>
    <li>The differences reflect how plants and animals obtain food and support themselves.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="muted">Model note: diagrams are simplified and not to scale; real cells contain many more organelles of each type.</p>
</SimulatorLayout>
